\section{Synthetic calibration}\label{app:synth}
All numbers in this section are computed on \emph{synthetic} Bradley--Terry data (no real comparisons): $M\in\{12,30\}$ models, $N$ from $2\times10^{3}$ to $1.28\times10^{5}$ comparisons, graph density $\{1,0.25\}$, tie rate $\{0,0.3\}$, three replicates per configuration; 11\,520 pairs with $1<z<12$. They fix the equivalence margin and the noise reference before any real result is read, and they are not evidence about real leaderboards.

\paragraph{Scaling.} A regression $\log k^{*}=a+b\log z+c\log N+d\log(\text{density})$ gives $b=1.11$ (deletion) and $1.13$ (flip) and $c=0.45$ and $0.43$, against $b=1$ and $c=\tfrac12$ if $k^{*}\propto z\sqrt N$ held exactly; residual standard deviation is $0.23$ and $0.20$ on the log scale. We therefore report the fitted exponents, not the proportionality.

\paragraph{The level of $Q$ under a correctly specified model.} Under exact refit, $Q$ is not bounded below by one. For deletion the mean of $\log Q$ is $-0.03$ ($Q\approx0.97$; from $0.90$ for sparse graphs without ties to $1.06$ for dense graphs with ties). For flips it is $+0.21$ and depends on density ($Q\approx1.08$--$1.10$ for sparse, $1.28$--$1.35$ for dense graphs, where the capacity of the most influential comparisons is used up). The level depends on the graph and on the family, which is why $Q$ is read only against the null of the same graph. The instance-level standard deviation of the mean of $\log Q$ within a configuration is $0.06$; the margin $0.15$ is $2.5$ times that.

\paragraph{First-order search.} The first-order estimate overshoots the verified count of deletion (median $k_{\mathrm{lin}}/k^{*}=1.26$, because influence grows as data are removed) and is accurate for flips ($0.97$).

\paragraph{Random deletion.} The probability that deleting a random fraction $f$ of the observed sample reverses a conclusion is $\Phi\big(-z\sqrt{(1-f)/f}\big)$, with root-mean-square error $0.010$ over more than 5\,000 cells; the variant $\Phi(-z\sqrt{1-f})$ has an error of $0.070$ and a bias of $-0.036$.

\paragraph{Search against exhaustive enumeration.} On 53 tiny problems ($M\in\{3,4\}$, $N=30$--$70$) the iterated search, the Frank--Wolfe proposer and the rounded-up relaxation all equal the exhaustive minimum in 53 of 53.

